WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.867
Bayesian networks are useful because they support several reasoning directions in one model.

2
00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:09.928
Predictive reasoning moves from possible causes toward likely effects.

3
00:00:09.928 --> 00:00:16.246
If a storm becomes more likely, a power interruption may also become more likely.

4
00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:22.564
If a server is under heavy load, a performance alert may become more likely.

5
00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:26.626
The model uses parent-to-child relationships to update expected outcomes.

6
00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:31.139
Diagnostic reasoning moves from an observed effect toward possible causes.

7
00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:40.164
If a power interruption is observed, the probability of storm damage, equipment fault, maintenance activity, or supplier outage may change.

8
00:00:40.164 --> 00:00:49.641
If a security alert is observed, the probability of benign misconfiguration and malicious activity may both change, depending on the model.

9
00:00:49.641 --> 00:00:57.313
Diagnostic reasoning is common in troubleshooting, medical screening at a high level, fraud review, and incident triage.

10
00:00:57.313 --> 00:01:00.472
It is not professional advice by itself.

11
00:01:00.472 --> 00:01:02.277
It is structured uncertainty.

12
00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:06.790
Intercausal reasoning occurs between alternative causes of a shared effect.

13
00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.498
This is where explaining away appears.

14
00:01:09.498 --> 00:01:15.816
Suppose two different causes can produce the same alarm: Equipment Fault and Sensor Fault.

15
00:01:15.816 --> 00:01:20.329
Observing the alarm may raise the probability of both causes.

16
00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:29.806
If later evidence confirms Sensor Fault, the probability of Equipment Fault may decrease because the confirmed sensor issue explains the alarm.

17
00:01:29.806 --> 00:01:33.416
The causes compete to explain the same effect.

18
00:01:33.416 --> 00:01:37.929
Explaining away is a probability shift, not a courtroom proof.

19
00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:42.442
The alternative cause may become less likely, but not impossible.

20
00:01:42.442 --> 00:01:46.052
In real models, multiple causes can occur together.

21
00:01:46.052 --> 00:01:50.565
Probability updates should be communicated with confidence, evidence, and uncertainty.

22
00:01:50.565 --> 00:02:00.042
Predictive, diagnostic, and intercausal reasoning help teams organize decisions, but responsible use requires remembering that updated probabilities are not absolute proof.

23
00:02:00.042 --> 00:02:03.201
This is especially helpful in operational settings.

24
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:11.324
A security alert might be caused by a misconfigured rule, benign user behavior, compromised credentials, or noisy telemetry.

25
00:02:11.324 --> 00:02:19.447
A fraud signal might come from ordinary travel, account takeover, data entry error, or a changed purchasing pattern.

26
00:02:19.447 --> 00:02:30.278
The network can update these explanations as evidence arrives, but the user still needs judgment, investigation, and appropriate escalation before acting on high-impact conclusions.
